USE bakenflake;

INSERT INTO blog_posts (title, slug, excerpt, content, image, author_id, is_published, published_at) VALUES

('How to Make the Perfect Croissant at Home', 'perfect-croissant-at-home',
 'The secrets behind those impossibly flaky, buttery layers — and yes, you can do it in your own kitchen.',
 '<p>There is perhaps no pastry more revered than the French croissant. Those golden, shattering layers that give way to a soft, buttery interior — it seems like magic. But it is not magic. It is patience, butter, and a technique called lamination.</p><h2>The Dough</h2><p>Start with a simple enriched dough: bread flour, sugar, salt, yeast, milk, and a touch of butter. Mix until smooth but do not overwork it. This is not bread — you want just enough gluten for structure, not chewiness. Wrap the dough tightly and refrigerate overnight.</p><h2>Lamination: The Heart of It All</h2><p>The next morning, pound a cold block of European-style butter into a flat square. Enclose it inside your dough like a letter in an envelope. Then roll and fold — three times, with 30-minute rests in the fridge between each turn. This creates 27 alternating layers of dough and butter.</p><h2>Shaping</h2><p>Roll the final dough into a large triangle-studded sheet. Cut tall, narrow triangles and roll each one from base to tip with gentle tension. Curve the ends inward to form the classic crescent shape. Let them proof at room temperature (not too warm, or the butter melts out) until they are visibly puffy and jiggly — about 2 hours.</p><h2>The Bake</h2><p>Egg wash generously, then bake at 400F for the first 10 minutes (to set the layers with steam), then reduce to 375F for another 8-10 minutes until deeply golden. The kitchen will smell extraordinary.</p>',
 'f4.jpg', NULL, 1, DATE_SUB(NOW(), INTERVAL 3 DAY)),

('The Science of Chocolate Tempering', 'science-of-chocolate-tempering',
 'Why does professional chocolate snap and shine? It all comes down to crystal structure.',
 '<p>If you have ever melted chocolate and let it set again, only to find it dull, streaky, and soft, you have experienced untempered chocolate. Tempering is the process of precisely controlling temperature to encourage the formation of Type V cocoa butter crystals — the only form that gives chocolate its signature snap, gloss, and smooth mouthfeel.</p><h2>The Crystal Problem</h2><p>Cocoa butter can crystallize in six different forms (Type I through Type VI). Only Type V has the properties we want: a melting point just below body temperature (which is why good chocolate melts on your tongue), a satisfying snap when you break it, and a shiny surface.</p><h2>The Table Method</h2><p>The classic technique: melt chocolate to 131F to erase all existing crystals, then pour two-thirds onto a cool marble slab. Work it with scrapers and palette knives, spreading and gathering it until it cools to about 80F. This seeding process creates a dense population of Type V crystals. Add it back to the remaining warm chocolate, bringing the whole batch to 88-90F for dark chocolate.</p><h2>At Our Bakery</h2><p>We temper chocolate daily for our ganache drips, chocolate-dipped cookies, and seasonal truffles. It is one of those techniques where understanding the science makes all the difference between homemade and professional results.</p>',
 'f5.jpg', NULL, 1, DATE_SUB(NOW(), INTERVAL 7 DAY)),

('A Love Letter to Sourdough Starters', 'love-letter-to-sourdough-starters',
 'They are alive, they are demanding, and they will change the way you think about bread forever.',
 '<p>My sourdough starter is named Ruth. She is four years old, lives in a jar on my counter, and has outlasted two apartments, one career change, and a global pandemic.</p><h2>The Beginning</h2><p>Starting a sourdough culture from scratch is an act of faith. You mix flour and water in a jar and wait. For the first two days, nothing happens. On day three, you might see some bubbles. Keep feeding. By day ten, your starter should be reliably doubling within 4-6 hours of feeding.</p><h2>The Relationship</h2><p>Maintaining a starter is like having a very simple pet. Feed it, keep it at a comfortable temperature, and it rewards you with extraordinary bread. Neglect it, and it gets sluggish but almost never dies.</p><h2>Why It Matters</h2><p>Commercial yeast makes bread rise. Sourdough makes bread alive. The long fermentation breaks down gluten into more digestible forms, develops complex flavors that commercial yeast cannot produce, and creates bread with a natural shelf life measured in days, not hours. Every loaf we bake at Bake''n Flake starts with a culture that has been nurtured for years.</p>',
 'f6.jpg', NULL, 1, DATE_SUB(NOW(), INTERVAL 12 DAY)),

('Behind the Counter: A Day in Our Baker''s Life', 'day-in-our-bakers-life',
 'The alarm goes off at 3:30 AM. By 4:00 AM, the ovens are warming. Here is what happens before you walk through our door.',
 '<p>Most people experience a bakery at 8 AM, when the cases are full and the coffee is brewing. But the real story begins in the dark, quiet hours when the rest of the city is asleep.</p><h2>3:30 AM — The Wake Up</h2><p>The alarm is not optional. Neither is the strong black coffee on the drive in. By 3:55, I am unlocking the back door of Bake''n Flake, flipping on the lights in a kitchen that still smells faintly of yesterday''s cinnamon rolls.</p><h2>4:00 AM — Ovens and Dough</h2><p>First task: turn on the ovens. They need 30 minutes to reach temperature. While they warm, I pull the overnight doughs from the retarder — sourdough loaves that have been cold-proofing for 14 hours, croissant dough that was laminated yesterday afternoon.</p><h2>5:30 AM — The Rush</h2><p>This is the most intense hour. Sourdough is coming out, croissants are going in, cookie dough is being portioned, cake layers from yesterday are being filled and frosted. Everything has a timer, and every timer matters.</p><h2>7:00 AM — Doors Open</h2><p>The cases are loaded, the espresso machine is dialed in, and the first customers are already lined up. Seeing someone''s face light up when they see a perfect golden croissant — that is why we wake up at 3:30 AM.</p>',
 'f7.jpg', NULL, 1, DATE_SUB(NOW(), INTERVAL 18 DAY)),

('Gluten-Free Baking That Actually Tastes Good', 'gluten-free-baking-that-tastes-good',
 'Forget the cardboard reputation. Modern gluten-free baking is delicious — if you know the right tricks.',
 '<p>Let us be honest: gluten-free baking has a reputation problem. For years, it meant dense, crumbly, vaguely sad approximations of real baked goods. But the science and the ingredients have come a long way.</p><h2>The Flour Blend Matters</h2><p>There is no single gluten-free flour that can replace wheat flour. The magic is in the blend. Our house mix combines white rice flour (for lightness), brown rice flour (for structure), tapioca starch (for chewiness and binding), and potato starch (for moisture).</p><h2>Fat is Your Friend</h2><p>Gluten-free baked goods benefit enormously from extra fat. Where a wheat recipe might use half a cup of butter, we often increase to three-quarters. The fat tenderizes the crumb, adds moisture, and carries flavor.</p><h2>Almond Flour: The Star</h2><p>For cookies and cakes, almond flour is transformative. It brings protein, fat, moisture, and a subtle nutty sweetness that wheat flour simply cannot match. Our almond flour donuts with rose petals are gluten-free, and most customers have no idea.</p><h2>Our Promise</h2><p>Every gluten-free item on our menu is developed to be great on its own terms, not as a substitute.</p>',
 'f8.jpg', NULL, 1, DATE_SUB(NOW(), INTERVAL 25 DAY)),

('The Story Behind Our Seasonal Specials', 'story-behind-seasonal-specials',
 'Why we change our menu with the seasons — and how we decide what makes the cut.',
 '<p>Four times a year, we retire some items and introduce new ones. It is one of the most creatively exciting parts of running Bake''n Flake.</p><h2>It Starts with Ingredients</h2><p>We do not brainstorm flavors in a vacuum. We start at the farmers market. When the first rhubarb appears in April, we know spring is really here. When figs show up in late August, we start dreaming about fall.</p><h2>The Testing Process</h2><p>A new seasonal item goes through at least five iterations. The first batch is usually a rough concept — does lavender work in a donut glaze? Does pumpkin belong in a croissant? Each version gets tasted by the whole team, and feedback is brutally honest.</p><h2>The Final Cut</h2><p>We usually test 12-15 items per season and launch 3-4. The ones that make it have to meet three criteria: they must be seasonal, they must be technically excellent, and they must make people smile.</p><h2>This Season</h2><p>Our current spring specials include the Peach Carrot Cake, the Winter Fruit Pavlova Roulade, and the Eggnog Glazed Donuts. Each one tells a story of experimentation, iteration, and the joy of seasonal ingredients at their peak.</p>',
 'f9.jpg', NULL, 1, DATE_SUB(NOW(), INTERVAL 30 DAY));
